    truth: "The culprit is Sota Kamiya, the lantern craftsman's apprentice.\nHe had devised his own improvement for lightening the lantern, and had been writing memos in the corner of the blueprint copy. He wanted to show his master but couldn't say it — until he finally took the drawing. It was hidden inside the great lantern being re-papered, and carried out to the workshop with the frame.\nThe decisive evidence: the fresh bamboo shavings beneath the lantern, the handwriting on the copy fragment, and the fact that only someone who carried the frame could hide something inside the lantern.\nThe blueprint was recovered safely. The master accepted Sota's improvement, and on the next great lantern, Sota's name will be carved among the craftsmen.",
    realStory: "The Kaminarimon great lantern burned down in 1865 and was missing for about 95 years. In 1960, Konosuke Matsushita — who had deep ties to Asakusa — donated it, and it was finally restored.\nSenso-ji, Tokyo's oldest temple, began in 628 with the legend of the Kannon statue in the net. Nakamise packs some 90 shops into about 250 m. The golden object at Azumabashi represents a 'flame', and together with the Asahi Breweries headquarters it has become Asakusa's new face.",
